{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"# Why the code-review severity axes are inert — root cause, zero-LLM (2026-08-12)\n\nOperator question before approving Phase 3: *\"consider why they're inert before acting on this.\nwe see very few code review findings.\"* Answer: **the axes are inert because the block decision\ndoes not read them for 98.5% of findings — not because they are uninformative, and not because\nthe reviewer finds little.** The ≤0.77% level-collapse flip rate recorded above is a *confounded*\nmeasurement. Corpus: all 1,545 `code_review` sidecar events in the tracker (3,839 findings),\nre-scored offline through the shipped `rebar.llm.review_kernel.decide.impact`.\n\n## 1. Distribution headline\n\n| metric | value |\n|---|---|\n| code-review runs | 1,545 |\n| findings | 3,839 — advisory 2,852 / dropped 913 / indeterminate 34 / **blocking 40** |\n| verdicts | PASS 1,500 (97.1%) · BLOCK 39 (2.5%) · INDETERMINATE 6 |\n| reviews with **0 blocking** | 1,506 / 1,545 = **97.5%** |\n| reviews with **0 findings at all** | 470 / 1,545 = 30.4% |\n| findings per review | median **2**, mean 2.48 (mode: 0, then 2, then 1) |\n| tier | LLM 3,772 · DET 59 |\n| **blocking by tier** | **DET 30 · LLM 2 · untiered 8** |\n\nThe operator's \"0–2 advisory non-blocking\" observation is exactly right and is the *normal* mode.\nBut the reviewer is **not** quiet: it emits 2.48 findings/run and self-labels **183 of them\n`major` or `critical`** (159 major + 24 critical). **Every one of those 183 landed in `advisory`\nexcept 2.** The LLM's total contribution to the block decision across the whole corpus is\n**2 findings in 1,545 reviews (0.13%)**. Blocking is effectively a *deterministic* gate:\n`bugfix-size-attestation` (27) + `sec`/`security` (10) + `secret-detection` (3).\n\nTop advisory criteria: tests 1,399 · maintainability 672 · correctness 617 · edge-cases 415 ·\nregression 284 · error-handling 172. Top dropped: docs 328 · tests 169 · maintainability 145.\n\n## 2. Root cause — two structural AND-gates, neither of which is severity\n\n**(i) Blocking is criterion-routed, not severity-routed.** Cross-tab of\n`block_threshold` × `blocking_enabled` × `tier`:\n\n```\n(0.95, False, LLM)  3353     <- 98.2% of scored findings: blocking DISABLED\n(0.54, True,  LLM)    51     <- the ONLY blocking-enabled criterion: `security`\n(0.90, False, LLM)    10     <- project.review-phase-boundaries, also disabled\n(None, None,  DET)    59     <- deterministic, no axes at all\n```\n\nOnly `security` (51 findings, 1.3%) is blocking-enabled. For the other 3,363, the severity axes\nare graded, serialized, and then discarded — the decision was fixed by routing before `impact()`\nwas consulted.\n\n**(ii) For those 3,363, the 0.95 threshold is functionally unreachable.** `impact()` is\n`(max(prod,debt) + blast + likelihood + reversibility) / 4`, each term ∈ {0.33, 0.67, 1.0}.\nExhaustive enumeration of all 432 attribute combinations:\n\n- **7 / 432 combos (1.6%) reach ≥ 0.95**, and **all 7 require the exact conjunction\n  `blast_radius=system` AND `likelihood=high` AND `reversibility=hard`** (plus prod or debt =\n  high). The threshold is not a severity dial — it is a hard 3-way AND on the rarest level of\n  three axes.\n- The next attainable score below 0.95 is **0.9175**. There is a **dead zone**: no finding can\n  ever score in (0.9175, 0.95).\n\nObserved marginals over 3,738 graded findings: `blast_radius=system` **92** · `likelihood=high`\n**246** · `reversibility=hard` **7** · **all-three conjunction: 0 (zero) occurrences.**\n\nEmpirical impact distribution: p50 **0.30**, p90 0.32, p99 0.60, **max 0.90**. Median gap to\nthreshold **0.65**; only 12 of 3,414 findings land within 0.10 of their threshold. The axes are\nnever exercised anywhere near the decision boundary because **the boundary is outside the\nobserved — and nearly outside the attainable — range.**\n\n**(iii) Even the one live path is degenerate.** All 7 findings that ever met their threshold\nscored *exactly* 0.54 (= the threshold), and only **2** became blocking: blocking on `security`\nadditionally requires `validity == 1.0`. Findings at impact 0.54 with validity 0.857 or 0.714\nstayed advisory. On that path `impact` behaves as a **binary** (0.54 fires vs ~0.30 doesn't),\nand the real discriminator is validity, not the ordinal ladder.\n\n## 3. Hypotheses adjudicated\n\n- **(a) \"few findings, mostly advisory — axes never exercised near the threshold\"** —\n  **TRUE but it is the symptom, not the cause.** Median gap-to-threshold 0.65 confirms the\n  non-exercise; §2 explains why it is structural, not a volume artifact. Adding 10x the findings\n  would not move it.\n- **(b) \"threshold/aggregation crushes variance — mean-of-attributes dilution\"\n  (idea `neat-sooty-termite`)** — **CONFIRMED, and the ticket understates it.** It is not merely\n  that the mean dilutes a high-severity axis; it is that mean-of-4 against a 0.95 cut collapses\n  the whole 5-axis ordinal space into a 3-way AND satisfied 0 times in 3,738 gradings, with a\n  0.9175→0.95 dead zone. **This is the root cause.**\n- **(c) \"reviewer genuinely finds little (fleet effect: clean changes)\"** — **REFUTED as the\n  primary cause.** 3,839 findings, 2.48/run, only 30% zero-finding runs, 183 self-labeled\n  major/critical. The reviewer finds plenty; what is rare is *blocking*, not *finding*. (A\n  fleet effect may well contribute to severity being mostly `minor` — 2,161 of 3,839 — but it\n  cannot explain 183 major/critical findings passing.)\n- **(d) \"reviewer under-reports (prompt/calibration reluctance)\"** — **REFUTED for severity\n  labelling.** The model applies `critical`/`major` freely and its labels are simply not\n  consulted by the gate. The weekly series shows **no reporting decay**: advisory/run runs\n  2.91 (W28), 2.07, 1.10, 1.73, 2.01, 2.20 (W33) — stationary, no step-down after any\n  calibration/prompt/routing commit. The rise in blocking (W32: 21, W33: 10, vs 0–8 before) is\n  fully attributable to the **DET** criteria `secret-detection` and `bugfix-size-attestation`\n  first appearing in W32 (commit `1920dc9fe5` \"Escalate big bug fixes to real review\") — i.e.\n  the gate got teeth from *deterministic* checks, not from LLM severity.\n  A separate *recall* question remains open (round-2 measured Nova Pro at ~50–57% blocking-\n  criteria recall on plan-review), but under-reporting is **not** why the axes are inert.\n\n**Verdict: (b) is the cause; (a) is its symptom; (c) and (d) are refuted as primary.**\n\n## 4. Recommendation on Phase 3 — do NOT proceed as scoped\n\nThe migration plan's post-audit note concludes \"Phase 3 is UNBLOCKED and LOW-RISK: code-review's\nordinal ladder carries almost no decision weight … so its migration or simplification can\nproceed.\" **That inference does not hold on this evidence.** The ≤0.77% flip rate measures a\nladder wired to a decision that is switched off — it is evidence of *disconnection*, not of\n*uninformativeness*. You cannot conclude an instrument is uncalibrated by reading it while it\nis unplugged. Migrating ordinals→kind-sets under that measurement would:\n\n1. **Bake in the defect.** A kind-set reconstruction validated against \"flips ≤0.77%\" is\n   validated against a constant function. Any kind set — including a random one — passes.\n2. **Spend the change budget on the wrong layer.** The ordinal encoding is not what makes\n   code-review pass everything; routing (`blocking_enabled=False` on 98.2%) and an unreachable\n   0.95 cut are.\n3. **Make the real defect harder to see later**, by rewriting the very fields that currently\n   document it.\n\n**Sequence instead:**\n\n- **P3.0 (zero-LLM, do first) — fix the threshold/aggregation, then re-measure.** Either lower\n  the non-security cut into the attainable range (the corpus p99 is 0.60; the 0.9175 ceiling\n  means anything above ~0.92 is a no-op), or replace mean-of-4 with a max/floor rule so one\n  `high`+`system` axis is not averaged away by three `easy`/`local` ones. Decide separately\n  and deliberately **which criteria should be blocking-enabled** — today it is exactly one.\n  This is a policy question for the operator, not a modelling one.\n- **P3.1 — re-run `code_decisiveness.py` against the corrected decision rule.** Only then does\n  the flip rate mean anything. If the axes still flip <1% *with a live threshold*, the\n  ordinal→kind-set migration is justified and cheap. If they flip materially, Phase 2's\n  \"reconstruct levels deterministically\" discipline applies here too.\n- **Phase 3 as currently written: hold.** Not \"pointless\" — **premature and, on the current\n  justification, actively wrong.**\n\nCheap intermediate sanity check, zero-LLM: replay the 183 existing `major`/`critical` LLM\nfindings through a candidate decision rule and inspect how many *should* have blocked. No new\ninference calls; the findings and their axes are already recorded.\n\n## 5. If you still want an under-reporting probe (not needed for Phase 3)\n\nUnder-reporting is refuted for *labelling*, but *recall* is untested for code-review. Cheapest\nconfirmation: replay N historically-defective changes through `review-code` and count catches.\nSourcing gold: commits that were later reverted, or whose ticket has a `discovered_from` child\nfiled as a bug against them. At the measured **$1.42/run** code-review cost, **N=20 ≈ $28**,\n**N=30 ≈ $43** (single epoch; double for 2-epoch stability). Recommend **N=20, one epoch, ~$28**\n— and only *after* P3.0, since a recall probe against a gate that cannot block measures the\nfinder in isolation and tells you nothing about the gate. **Not run; approval required.**\n\n*(Analysis-only. Zero LLM calls. Scripts are ad-hoc re-scores through the shipped `impact()`;\nmethod matches round2/code_decisiveness.py with the field-shape correction applied.)*"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786532129700991001,"uuid":"d447604d-e543-4582-8ed2-15a7af0ff944"}